rred to as ILO is perhaps  the only international labor body that I  in a conference that i know that's  tripartite and its structure  there we have representatives of the  government representatives of management  and representatives of labor meeting in  a common forum and each speaks very  freely and independently upon all  matters that come before that body well  mr. Wilkins uh the Russians have  recently rejoined the international  labor organization and how does a labor  there treat its employers do you  differentiate in the international body  between labor and employer in Russia the  government know between the government  and the employer I think I don't even  more difficult that is one of the  principal objections raised to the  employer and employee delegates from  soviet russia and her satellites that  they have no such thing as free  employers are free employees and the  peculiar thing about it that I observed  at the conference was that at no time  during the conference did the employer  delegate or the employee delegate from  Russia are her satellites vote in a  different manner than the  representatives of the Russian  government on its settlement in the  American delegation tend to stick  together also in his views no the  frequently the government delegate from  the united states voted one way and the  employer delegate voted another way  frequently the employee delegated voted  contra to the way the delegate from the  government voted but the delegates from  soviet russia always voted solidly as a  block mr. Wilkins what is the  international labor organization trying  to do and how can the United States  supply itself to its aims Wow and the  primary objective of the ILO is to  improve their working conditions and  living conditions of laboring people we  recognize that the standards in the  United States of America are  considerably higher than they are in the  other nations of the world I mean  particularly the underdeveloped  countries of the world but it is to our  advantage to have working conditions  improved in those countries because as  working conditions are improved in the  other developed countries of the world  our working conditions are better that  is to say that the competition between  the workers  the other developed countries and our  and the United States of America are not  so keen there there were not a parallel  build it did Russia during this last  meeting of the International Labor  Organization show any tendency to  compromise or two or to go along with  the other countries or was it pretty  much in opposition Russia the delegates  from the Soviet countries never  compromised on anything they were had a  man in their positions of course they  pictured labor and soviet russia as  being a very fine thing but they never  compromise they took their position and  stood by even in defeat well did we ever  compromise on any of our positions mr.  Wilkins yeah I don't think we ever  compromised except in certain instances  when the matter came up of giving the  delegates from soviet russian or  satellites deputy positions on  committees we voted for that because  deputy on the committee's of the ILO all  they sat in all the meetings never had  any vote at all mr. Wilkers you think  that these overseas labor unions are  important factors in the great political  struggles of the Cold War I think the  labor unions overseas are very important  I think labor is one of the facets by  which we either spread our stop  communism where you have a satisfied  labor force I think you do not have a  fertile soil for communism to develop it  how would you evaluate the role of our  American labor unions in international  affairs and in regard to the ILO is work  I think our trade unions particularly  they have a valency io play a very  important role in developing better  relations between the working people of  the world certainly the labor unions of  the foreign countries are more kindly  disposed to listen to what is said by  American labor unions I think there's  some time suspicious of what the  delegates from the government say tell  them mr. Wilkins you think we're doing  enough to help and encourage the  labor movements overseas in the free  countries I don't think we're doing  everything that we opted to do it seems  to me when the feel of technical  assistance we are doing some things but  I think we ought to do considerably more  as you know mr. lisser that  three-fourths of the peoples of the  world and non-white and they sometimes  look with suspicion upon the attitude of  the American government we tried to  point out at the conference in Geneva  that the United States government  doesn't expect any particular favors  from a nation that it helps it only  helps these nations trying to make them  strong enough to be free but mr. will  there any exhibition is there anything  we can actually do that our government  could properly do to increase and  encourage labor organizations and other  countries I think we can do a number of  things the principle I think the thing I  think that we can do is to send labour  technicians particularly to the foreign  countries and where they might learn how  we operate between management and labor  wouldn't we make great enemies among  certain elements of management in those  countries I do not think if we make  enemies certainly there might be some  feeling of mistrust but generally I  think we've work in a good feel I don't  know whether you know it or not but we  have foreigners who come to the Labor  Department in Washington to study our  methods management relations between  labor and management we have them study  our field of workmen compensation law  and other laws so that they can go back  to their countries and try to put into  effect some of the principles which we  have established in this country mr.  Wilkins I was thinking about one of the  problems here what about segregation and  government work here aren't y